Meta has agreed to pay what could amount to billions of dollars and to implement platform usage limitations on young users in a lawsuit brought by US states. The challenge revolved around addiction issues and overuse.

Meta, the parent company of Facebook and Instagram, has agreed to pay as much as $16.7 billion (roughly €14.3 billion) and to impose new limits on how teenagers use its social media sites as part of a lawsuit in California brought by a number of US states .&#160;

The proposal was filed in federal court on Wednesday and seeks to resolve claims from a coalition of 47 US states in total.&#160;

As well as the financial damages, it foresees Meta introducing various limitations and changes designed to improve child safety and discourage or prevent overuse and social media activity overnight or during school hours.

"Meta has agreed to make massive transformations that will reduce the risk of harm from its platforms — and will do it within months," California Attorney General Rob Bonta said in a statement as the settlement was announced.&#160;

He called the agreement "real change, real transparency, and real enforceable protections for children."&#160;

The filing states that the agreement does not amount to any admission of liability or wrongdoing from Meta, which had consistently denied the allegations.&#160;

Virginia Attorney General Jay Jones, however, argued that Meta had intentionally put young people's health at risk and that the changes helped to stop this.&#160;

"For years, Meta intentionally deceived the public about the addictive and harmful design features that have wreaked havoc&#160;on youth mental health," Jones said, arguing that the settlement "will put an end to these dangerous practices and deliver meaningful relief that will protect children from online harm."&#160;

Meta is proposing a series of usage limitations for people under the age of 18, including a "hard cap" on the daily total time spent on their sites.&#160;

The cumulative all-day cap is to be set at 2 hours of daily use across Meta's various platforms. Parents will be authorized to lift the restriction. Meta said that in cases where it detects distinct users with multiple accounts, it would apply across them.&#160;

Beween midnight and 6 a.m., underage users will also be suspended from the sites.&#160;

In what Meta calls "School Mode," notifications will be muted by default between 8 a.m and 3 p.m., with teens no longer receiving push notifications during these hours, "except for direct messages and alerts about their account safety or security."

