Rescue teams are searching for survivors after a glacial collapse triggered catastrophic floods in Nepal and Tibet. Hundreds of foreign tourists are missing.

The death toll from catastrophic flash floods along the border between&#160; Nepal &#160;and Tibet has risen to at least 165, while nearly 1,400 people were missing.

Rescue teams were searching&#160;for survivors on Thursday, a day after a glacial collapse sent a huge wall of mud and rock barreling through the Himalayan communities, sweeping way entire villages.

Nepal's National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Authority said 826 people were missing in the country, almost 600 of them foreign nationals.

In Tibet, Chinese broadcaster CCTV reported 558 people missing in Gyirong county, including 260 foreigners. Three deaths had been confirmed there as of Thursday morning, with two villagers rescued.

The disaster struck Wednesday when a glacial collapse triggered a flood in the Lhende Khola river, a tributary of the Bhotekoshi, in a corridor popular with trekkers and pilgrims connecting Kathmandu with Lhasa.&#160;

Nepal police spokesperson Abi Narayan Kafle told Reuters the death toll was expected to keep climbing: "This will go up, as we have resumed the search and more bodies are expected to be recovered."

Sunil Sharma, a tourism board spokesperson, told the AP news agency that 133 of the missing were Indian pilgrims traveling to Mount Kailash in Tibet, a site sacred to Hindus, alongside 47 Americans, 34 Australians, 33 Britons and 24 Canadians.&#160;

Reuters cited a slightly different breakdown from police and tourism officials, putting the missing tourists at 177 from India, 63 from the United States, 34 from Australia, 33 from Britain and 25 from Canada.&#160;

